{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "kind": "lesson",
  "items": [
    {
      "external_id": "C12-CHEM-CH19-19.4",
      "chapter": "pk-punjab/xii/chemistry/c12-chem/ch19",
      "topic": "pk-punjab/xii/chemistry/c12-chem/ch19/19.4",
      "title": "19.4 Nomenclature of Aliphatic Organic Compounds",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "2d720c54-503b-58ec-b5b1-3307f222f40c",
          "pdf_from": 41,
          "pdf_to": 45,
          "note": "Topic 19.4 with the classes 1-13 and Quick Check 19.3, PDF pages 41-44."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Since, millions of organic compounds are found in nature and hundreds and thousands of new compounds are being synthesized every year, so a need was felt to name these compounds according to their structures. A set of rules was thus formulated by IUPAC (International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry) for systematic nomenclature of organic compounds. The systematic or IUPAC name of an organic compound is based on the parent hydrocarbon. Each name consists of the components listed below."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "i) Stem or Root"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "It gives the longest continuous carbon chain including the carbon bearing the functional group. Lowest possible number is given to the carbon bearing the functional group. If the carbon atoms are joined in a ring, the name is started by “cyclo”."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "ii) Suffix"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "There are two types of suffixes:"
          },
          {
            "type": "list",
            "ordered": true,
            "items": [
              "Primary suffix, also called suffix of stem or root. It indicates whether the parent chain is saturated or unsaturated.",
              "Secondary suffix, also called suffix of the key functional group. It gives family name of the compound."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "iii) Prefix"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "It gives the name of the side chain (alkyl group) or any substituent (e.g some functional group like halo, nitro, etc.) along with number of carbon atom to which it is attached."
          },
          {
            "type": "diagram",
            "$asset": "c12-chem-ch19-fig-name-parts",
            "alt": "A chain of four shapes: a yellow circle 'Prefix', a blue hexagon 'Stem/Root name', a blue hexagon 'Primary Suffix (Suffix of Stem/root)' and a yellow circle 'Secondary Suffix (Suffix of F.G.)'. Arrows lead from Prefix to 'substituent and its position on the parent chain', from Stem to 'No. of carbon atoms in the parent chain', from Primary Suffix to 'saturation or unsaturation in the parent chain (an, en, yn)' and from Secondary Suffix to 'family name'.",
            "caption": "Components of a systematic (IUPAC) name (original drawing after the textbook's unnumbered figure)."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "If prefix is absent, then systematic name will consist of root and suffix."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Examples:"
          },
          {
            "type": "diagram",
            "$asset": "c12-chem-ch19-fig-naming-examples",
            "alt": "Two worked examples. First: Cl-CH2-CH2-CH2-C(=O)-H with the chain carbons numbered 4, 3, 2, 1 from the left, and the name split in a box as 4-chloro | but | an | al, the parts labelled prefix, stem, suffix of stem and suffix of F.G. Second: CH3-C(CH3)=CH-CH2-OH numbered 4, 3, 2, 1, the name split as 3- methyl | but- | 2-en- | 1-ol with the same four labels.",
            "caption": "Examples: 4-chlorobutanal and 3-methylbut-2-en-1-ol, split into prefix, stem and suffixes (original drawing after the textbook's unnumbered figure)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "The first printed example numbers a four-carbon chain but names it '4-chloro prop an al'; the stem for four carbons is 'but', so the name is 4-chlorobutanal, as drawn above."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Keeping in view, the above procedure for giving systematic or IUPAC names to various organic compounds, following different classes will be discussed one by one:"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "1. Alkyl halides (CₙH₂ₙ₊₁X) (–C–X)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is halogenoalkane (more precisely monohaloalkane)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{chloromethane}}{\\ce{CH3-Cl}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{2-bromopropane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH(Br)-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{3-methyl-1-iodobutane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-I}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–Cl (chloromethane);  CH₃–CH(Br)–CH₃ (2-bromopropane);  CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–I (3-methyl-1-iodobutane)",
            "book_errors": [
              "3-methyl-1-iodobutane"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "The substituent prefixes are written in alphabetical order (iodo before methyl), so the name of CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–I is 1-iodo-3-methylbutane."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "2. Alcohols (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂O) (–C–OH)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkanol."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{methanol}}{\\ce{CH3-OH}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{propan-1-ol}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH2-OH}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{2-methylpropan-2-ol}}{\\ce{CH3-C(OH)(CH3)-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–OH (methanol);  CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–OH (propan-1-ol);  CH₃–C(OH)(CH₃)–CH₃ (2-methylpropan-2-ol)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "3. Aldehydes (CₙH₂ₙO) (–C(=O)–H)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkanal."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{methanal}}{\\ce{H-C(=O)-H}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{ethanal}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-H}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{3-methylbutanal}}{\\ce{CH4-CH(CH3)-CH2-C(=O)-H}}",
            "text_alt": "H–C(=O)–H (methanal);  CH₃–C(=O)–H (ethanal);  CH₄–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–C(=O)–H (3-methylbutanal)",
            "book_errors": [
              "CH₄–CH(CH₃)",
              "CH4-CH(CH3)"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "Carbon 4 of 3-methylbutanal is a methyl group, CH₃, not CH₄: the formula is CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–C(=O)–H."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "4. Ketones (CₙH₂ₙO) (–C–C(=O)–C–)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkanone"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{propanone}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{pentan-2-one}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH2-C(=O)-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–C(=O)–CH₃ (propanone);  CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–C(=O)–CH₃ (pentan-2-one)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "5. Carboxylic acid (CₙH₂ₙO₂) (–C(=O)–OH)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkanoic acid"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{methanoic acid}}{\\ce{H-C(=O)-OH}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{ethanoic acid}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-OH}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{2-methylbutanoic acid}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH(CH3)-C(=O)-OH}}",
            "text_alt": "H–C(=O)–OH (methanoic acid);  CH₃–C(=O)–OH (ethanoic acid);  CH₃–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–C(=O)–OH (2-methylbutanoic acid)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "6. Acid amides (CₙH₂ₙ₋₁ONH₂) (–C(=O)–NH₂)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkanamide"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{ethanamide}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-NH2}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{2-methylpropanamide}}{\\ce{CH3-CH(CH3)-C(=O)-NH2}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{N-methylbutanamide}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH2-C(=O)-NH-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–C(=O)–NH₂ (ethanamide);  CH₃–CH(CH₃)–C(=O)–NH₂ (2-methylpropanamide);  CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–C(=O)–NH–CH₃ (N-methylbutanamide)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "7. Acid halides (CₙH₂ₙ₋₁OX) (–C(=O)–X)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkanoyl halides"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{ethanoyl chloride}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-Cl}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{propanoyl chloride}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-C(=O)-Cl}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–C(=O)–Cl (ethanoyl chloride);  CH₃–CH₂–C(=O)–Cl (propanoyl chloride)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "8. Esters (CₙH₂ₙO₂) (–C(=O)–O–C)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkyl alkanoate"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{methyl ethanoate}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-O-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{ethyl ethanoate}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-O-CH2-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{propyl propanoate}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-C(=O)-O-CH2-CH2-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–C(=O)–O–CH₃ (methyl ethanoate);  CH₃–C(=O)–O–CH₂–CH₃ (ethyl ethanoate);  CH₃–CH₂–C(=O)–O–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ (propyl propanoate)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "9. Acid anhydrides (–C(=O)–O–C(=O)–)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkanoic anhydride"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{ethanoic anhydride}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-O-C(=O)-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{ethanoic propanoic anhydride}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-O-C(=O)-CH2-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–C(=O)–O–C(=O)–CH₃ (ethanoic anhydride);  CH₃–C(=O)–O–C(=O)–CH₂–CH₃ (ethanoic propanoic anhydride)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "10. Ethers (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂O) (–C–O–C–)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkoxyalkane."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{methoxymethane}}{\\ce{CH3-O-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{ethoxyethane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-O-CH2-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{methoxyethane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-O-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{1-methoxy-2 methylpropane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-O-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–O–CH₃ (methoxymethane);  CH₃–CH₂–O–CH₂–CH₃ (ethoxyethane);  CH₃–CH₂–O–CH₃ (methoxyethane);  CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–O–CH₃ (1-methoxy-2 methylpropane)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "11. Amines (CₙH₂ₙ₊₁NH₂)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "There are three types of amines"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "a) Primary Amines (–C–NH₂). The IUPAC name is Alkanamine"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{ethanamine}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-NH2}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{propan-1-amine}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH2-NH2}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{propan-2-amine}}{\\ce{CH3-CH(NH2)-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CH₂–NH₂ (ethanamine);  CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–NH₂ (propan-1-amine);  CH₃–CH(NH₂)–CH₃ (propan-2-amine)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "b) Secondary Amines (–C–NH–R). The IUPAC name is N-Alkylalkanamine"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{N-methylpropan-1-amine}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH2-NH2-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{N-methylpropan-2-amine}}{\\ce{CH3-CH(CH3)-NH-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–NH₂–CH₃ (N-methylpropan-1-amine);  CH₃–CH(CH₃)–NH–CH₃ (N-methylpropan-2-amine)",
            "book_errors": [
              "NH₂–CH₃",
              "NH2-CH3"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "In a secondary amine the nitrogen carries one hydrogen: N-methylpropan-1-amine is CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–NH–CH₃. With NH₂ the nitrogen would have four bonds."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "c) Tertiary amines (–C–N(R)–R). The IUPAC name may be (R=R) N,N – Dialkylakanamine or (R≠R) N – alkyl – N – alkyalkanamine",
            "book_errors": [
              "Dialkylakanamine",
              "alkyalkanamine"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "The class names are N,N-dialkylalkanamine and N-alkyl-N-alkylalkanamine."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{N,N-dimethylpropan-1-amine}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH2-N(CH3)-CH3}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{N-ethyl-N-methylpropan-1-amine}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH2-N(CH3)-CH2-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–N(CH₃)–CH₃ (N,N-dimethylpropan-1-amine);  CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–N(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃ (N-ethyl-N-methylpropan-1-amine)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "12. Alkyl cyanides (CₙH₂ₙ₋₁N) (–C≡N)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is alkanenitrile"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{ethanenitrile}}{\\ce{CH3-CN}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{2-methylpropaneritrile}}{\\ce{CH3-CH(CH3)-CN}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CN (ethanenitrile);  CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CN (2-methylpropaneritrile)",
            "book_errors": [
              "propaneritrile"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "The name is 2-methylpropanenitrile."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "13. Nitroalkanes (CₙH₂ₙ₊₁NO₂) (–C–NO₂)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The IUPAC name is nitroalkane"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{nitromethane}}{\\ce{CH3-NO2}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{nitropropane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH2-NO2}} \\qquad \\underset{\\text{2-nitropropane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH(NO2)-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–NO₂ (nitromethane);  CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–NO₂ (nitropropane);  CH₃–CH(NO₂)–CH₃ (2-nitropropane)"
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "note",
            "title": "Quick Check 19.3",
            "text": "a) What are the systematic names of the following?\ni) (CH₃)₃COH\nii) CH₃CCl₃\niii) CH₂=CH–CH₂–OH\niv) CH₃COC₆H₅\nv) CH₂ = C(CH₃)COOCH₃\nb) Draw the structures of the following.\ni) 2 - methylbutanal\nii) prop -2- ylmethanoate\niii) phenylethanoic acid\niv) cyclohexanone\nv) 3 - methylpentanal"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
